# Ch453 Diseases of the Spinal Cord

- **Clnical manifestations of spinal cord diseases** - debellitating as small cross-section densely packed by longitudinal tracts:
  - <u>Motor weakness</u> - quadriplegia or paraplegia
  - <u>Sensory deficits</u> - usually a/w sensory level

## Approach to the Patient with Spinal Cord Disease

- **Terminology:**
  - <u>Conus medularis</u> - terminal end of the spinal cord at the lumbar levels
  - <u>Filum terminales</u> - fibrous extension of the cord extending from lubar levels to the coccyx
- **Physiological anatomy of the spinal cord** - tubular extension of the CNS contained within the bony spinal canal, extending from the medulla to the conus medullaris:
  - White matter tracts containing ascending sensory tracts and descending sensory tracts located peripherally
  - Cell bodies clustered in inner region in a four-leaf clover shape surrounding the central canal (extension of 4th ventricle)
  - Surounded by leptomeninges, containing the pia, arachnoid, and dura matter (CSF found in between pia and arachnoid mater)
  - Forms a long, oval tube expanded in the cervical and lumbar regions corresponding to dense innervation to the UL and LL respectively
  - Contains 31 segments defined by an exiting ventral motor root and entering dorsal sensory root
- **Transverse anatomy of the spinal cord:** 
![](../img/transverse_view_of_spinal_cord.png)
- **Relationship between spinal level and vertebral level** - growth of cord lacks behind that of spinal level during embryonic development and explains unique presentation of myelopathic disorders:
  - Spinal cord terminates at T12/L1 junction, and by principle, <u>lower spinal nerves may lag take an increasing downward course to exit via their respective neuroforamina</u>
  - C1-7 nerve roots exit above the same-numbered vertebrae, while the others exit below the same-numbered vertebrae (as there are 8 cervical roots but 7 cervical VBs)

  
  ![](../img/spinal_cord_level_corresponding_to_vertebral_body.png)
- **Suggestive findings of a spinal cord lesion** - sensory, motor and autonomic impairment explained by a transverse lesion:
  - <u>Sensory level</u> - sought by asking assessing pinprick sensation fro proximal legs and lower trunk to the neck bilaterally:
    - Unilateral cord lesion - clinical spinal level may be discrepent by 1-2 levels (usually below) as 1st order neurons may ascend 1-2 levels to the contralateral tract before synapsing
    - Bilateral cord lesion - clinical spinal level usually at the level at the lesion
  - <u>Motor weakness</u> - trasection of corticospinal and other motor tracts results in:
    - Paraplegia or quadriplegia depending on the level of transection
    - Hyperrefleixia (heightened deep tendon reflexes)
    - Upgoing plantars (and other myelopathic signs such as inverted brachoradialis and Hoffman's sign)
    - Spasicity (typically late but completes the UMN syndrome)
  - <u>Autonomic disturbances</u>:
    - Absent sweating below implicated cord level
    - Bladder, bowel, and sexual dysfunction
- **Assessment of the level of of spinal cord lesion** - uppermost level of spinal cord lesion can be localised by attension of segmental signs reflecting root involvement:
  - Band of hyperalgesia or hyperpathia (Radicular pain) at the upper end of sensory disturbance
  - LMN signs (fasciculation, atrophy, muted reflexes) corresponding to the sensory level
- **Segmental signs are only useful in chronic myelopathies as:**
  - State of spinal shock (days to weeks) during acute transverse lesions results in limbs to be flaccid rather than spastic, and may be mistakened for 1) acute polyneuropathy, or 2) widespread anterior horn disease
  - Much more easier to interpret and delineate level of lesion when they occur w/ signs of long tract damage
- **Features of cervical cord lesion** - 1) high cervical lesions results quadriplegia and diaphragmatic weakness, 2) mid-to-low cervical lesions results in paraplegia w/ myotomal involvement of the UL:
  - <u>C5-C6 lesion</u> - weakness on flexion at elbow + hyporreflexia of bicep reflexes
  - <u>C7 lesions</u> - weakness on extension at elbow, and extension of wrists and fingers (sparing the above) + hyporreflexia of triceps reflex
  - <u>C8 lesions</u> - weakness of flexion of wrist and fingers (saring the above)
  - <u>Horner syndrome</u> - miosis, ptosis, and hypohidrosis may accompany any C-spine lesion
- **Features of thoracic cord lesion** - level determined by sensory level on the trunk:
  - <u>T4 lesion</u> - sensory level at the nipples
  - <u>T10 lesion</u> - sensory level of umbilicus (w/ Beevor sign, i.e. uppward movement on abdominal wall contraction as only lower abdominal muscles impaired)
- **Features of lumbar cord lesion**:
  - <u>L2-L4 lesion</u> - impaired flexion and extension of thigh, extension of knee + loss of patellar reflex
  - <u>L5-S1 lesion</u> - impaired flexion of the knee, movement of the ankle (dorsi-flexion, plantar-flexion, inversion, and eversion) + loss of ankle jerks
- **Features of conus medullaris syndrome** (S2-S5):
  - Bilateral saddle anesthesia (S3-S5)
  - Bladder (urinary retention), bowel (incontinence w/ laxed anal tone), and sexual (impotenence) dysfunction
  - Loss of bulbocarvenosus (S2-S4) and anal (S4-S5) reflexes
  - Muscle strength is usually preserved (unless mixed cauda equina sydnrome)
- **Longuitudinal tracts may traverse ipsilateral or contralateral to the side they innervate or supply:**
  - Most longutidinal tracts traverse along the ipsilateral to the side they innervate, including the dorsal column and the descending corticospinal tracts (decussation occurs higher up)
  - Afferent fibers mediating pain and temperature ascend in spinothalamic tract contralateral to the side they supply
  - The anatomical configuration produces characteristic spinal cord syndromes providing clues to the underlying disease process (esp. Brown-Sequard Syndrome)
- **Brown-Sequard Hemicord syndrome** - hemicord transection, most commonly partial forms rather than fully developed syndrome:
  - Ipsilateral segmental signs including radicular pain, hyporreflexia etc, usually one or two levels below the lesion
  - Ipsilateral weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement (flaccid acutely, and spastic in chronic phase)
  - Ipsilateral loss of proprioception due to dorsal column involvement
  - Contralateral loss of pain and temperature (below the level of segmental signs) due to spinothalamic tract involvement
- **Central Cord Syndrome** - due to preferential damage to 1) gray matter cells, 2) decussating fibres to the spinothalamic tract:
  - Quadriplegia with <u>arm weakness out of proportion to leg weakness</u>
  - Dissociative sensory loss in cape distribution (shoulder, lower neck, upper trunk):
    - Impaired pain and temperature sensation
    - Preserved light touch and proproception
  - Most commonly due to 1) C-spiine injury, 2) syringomyelia, or 3) intrinsic cord tumours
- **Anterior Cord Syndrome** - invariably caused by <u>anterior spinal artery ischaemia</u>, with bilateral cord damage that spares the dorsal columns:
  - Motor, sensory, and autonomic dyfunction below the level of the lesion
  - Striking retained proprioception due to sparing of the dorsal columns
- **Foramen Magnum Syndrome** - SOL near the foramen magnum interrupting decussating pyramidal tracts of the legs (N.B. caudal to decussation for the arms):
  - Quadriplegia that progresses in an "around-the-clock" pattern beginning theoretically in any four limbs, most commonly progresses from ipsilateral UL, ipsilateral LL, contralateral LL, and contralateral UL
  - Suboccipital pain/ headache radiating to neck and shoulders
- **Intramedullary vs Extramedullary Syndromes** - differentiation based on the progression of Sx and Sx burden:
  - <u>Extramedullary syndromes</u> - arises from outside the cord compressing the cord or its vascular supply:
    - Prominent radicular pain as often a component of radiculopathy with compressive Sx
    - Early sacral sensory loss (saddle parasthesia) due to the laminated configuraiton of the Spinothalamic tract (Sacral levels outer, Cervical levels inner)
    - Early spastic weakness and autonomic dysfunction due to early involvement of the corticospinal tracts
    - Further differentiation btween extradural (usu. malignant cord compression), and intradural mass (us. benign like neurofibroma) based on rate of progression of Sx (malignant masses progress more rapidly while benign mass progress indolently)
  - <u>Intramedullary syndromes</u> - arises from substance of the cord:
    - Pain is poorly localised and is usually burning in nature, rather than a radicular pain
    - Relatively "sacral sparing" reflecting laminated configuration of spinothalamic tract
    - Spastic weakness is late as the corticospinal tract occurs later
- **Compressive vs non-compressive myelopathy:**
  - <u>Compressive myelopathy</u> (esp. malignancy or abscess) - **antecedent warning signs** such as neck or back pain, bladder disturbances, and **sensory Sx preceeding development of paralysis** (rule does not necessary apply to acute processes such as subluxation or haemorrhage)
  - <u>Noncompressive myelopathy</u> - full blown myelopathy without antecedent symptoms
- **Most common causes of compressive myelopathy:**
  - Tumor (neoplastic spinal cord compression)
  - Epidural abscess
  - Hematoma
  - Herniated disk
  - Spondylitic vertebral pathology
- **Most common causes of noncompressive myelopathy** - intrinsic to the cord:
  - Vascular
  - Inflammatory
  - Infective
  - Metabolic
- **DDx of treatable spinal cord disorders** - VINDICATE approach: 
![](../img/treatable_spinal_cord_disorders.png)

## Acute and Subacute Spinal Cord Diseases

- **Clinical features of acute or subacute spinal cord disease** - evolution of Sx over days and weeks:
  - <u>Focal neck or back pain</u> - especially for compressive myelopathies, and may be radicular in nature, where these Sx may preceed onset of sensory, motor or sphincteric Sx
  - <u>Various combinations of sensory, motor and sphincteric disturbances</u>:
    - Sensory disturbances - sensory loss or parasthesia below the level of lesion associated w/ a sensory level (see above for differentiation between an intramedullary or extramedullary lesion)
    - Motor disturbances - may be initially flaccid and arreflexic due to spinal shock (mistakened to be a polyneuropathy), but spasticity and hyperreflexia supervenes over days or weeks (rarely persistent arareflexia indicates necrosis over multiple segments)
    - Sphincteric disturbances - urinary retention and fecal incontinence
  - <u>Other differentiating features</u> - e.g. constitutional Sx, systems review in Hx important in delineating the underlying cause
- **DDx of acute or subacute spinal cord disease:**
  - <u>Peripheral neuropathy</u> - often considered if describes an ascending parasthesia beginning at the feet:
    - Persistence of flaccid paralysis and arreflexia might suggest peripheral neuropathy but spinal shock may mimic this picture
    - Presence of concomittent bladder disturbances, sensory levels and segmental signs (if long tract signs +ve) confirms a spinal pathology
- **DDx of acute or subacute spinal cord disease** - compressive vs noncompressive myelopathy:
  - **Compressive myelopathy** - traumatic vs non-traumatic:
    - <u>Traumatic</u> - spinal cord injury
    - <u>Non-traumatic</u>:
      - Neoplastic spinal cord compression
      - Spinal epidural abscess
      - Spinal epidural haematoma
      - Haematomyelia
      - Acute spondylytic myelopathy
  - **Noncompressive myelopathy:**
    - Vascular - spinal cord infarction, AV malformation and dural fistula
    - Inflammatory or immune-mediated myelopathies (i.e. myelitis) - MS, NOSD, other neuroinflammatory diseases (e.g. Anti-MOD, ADEM), acute infectious myelitis, post-infectious myelitis, sarcoidosis, systemic autoimmune disease (APLS and SLE, Sjogren's syndrome, Behchet's disease, vasculitis, CLIPPERS)
- **Evaluation of Myelopathy:** 
![](../img/evaluation_of_myelopathy_harrisons.png)

### Compressive Myelopathies

1.  Neoplastic Spinal Cord Compression

    - **Definition** - clinical presentation of acute or subacute spinal cord compression caused by a neoplasm, further classified into epidural neoplasm and intradural neoplasm
    - **Etiology of neoplastic spinal cord compression:**
      - <u>Epidural mass</u> - usually metastasis to the adjacent vertebral body (high proportion of marrow) from a distant primary, characteristically to the thoracic vertebrae:
        - Prostate
        - Breast
        - Lung
        - Kidney
        - Ovary
        - Lymphoma
        - Myeloma
      - <u>Intradural mass</u>:
        - Common causes - meningioma, neurofibroma
        - Uncommon causes - chordoma, lipoma, dermoid, or sarcoma
    - **Intramedullary spinal cord tumours** - non-compressive, usually presents as central cord syndrome or Brown-Sequard Syndrome:
      - Ependymomas
      - Hemangioblastomas
      - Low-grade astrocytomas
      - Secondary metastasis (usually in advanced metastatic disease but not nearly as frequent as brain metastasis)
    - **Common site of metastatic spinal cord compression:**
      - <u>Thoracic spine</u> - most commonly inolved
      - <u>Lumbar spine</u> - rarely involved in metastatic spinal cord compression except for **prostate cancer** and **ovarian cancer** possibly by venous drainage through the **Baxton's plexus**
    - **Clinical features of neoplastic spinal cord compression** - relatively rapid progression from antecedent Sx to acute presentation w/ compression:
      - **Back pain** - usually an initial Sx of spinla metastasis (absence of pain is rare):
        - <u>Site</u> - commonly over thoraci spine but lumbar spine for certain primries (see above)
        - <u>Onset</u> - insiduous onset but rather rapidly progression, new onset of persistent non-mechanical back painespecially in thoracic spine should raise suspcion (?DDx TB)
        - <u>Progression</u> - progressive pain
        - <u>Provocating and relieving factors</u>:
          - Provoked by movement, coughing, or sneezing
          - Not relieved by rest (in fact characteristically awakens patients at night)
        - <u>Quality</u> - non-mechanical, aching, sharp, radiating pain
        - <u>Radiation</u> - localised to back, **radiating if a/w radiculopathy or myelopathy**
        - <u>Severity</u> - rather persistent and severe affecting sleep, may be the predominant manifestation
      - **Features of spinal cord compression** (myelopathy) - characteristically sensory Sx (e.g. parasthesia) before paralysis, and w/ early onset of 1) saddle parasthesia, 2) spastic paralysis, and 3) sphincteric disturbances:
        - <u>Muscle weakness</u> - symmetrical, e.g. bilateral LL weakness with increased (or absent) jerks
        - <u>Sensory deficits</u> - sensory level w/ sensory changes developing in dermatomes below the level of compression
        - <u>Spincteric dysfunction</u> - manifests as AROU or
    - **Ix:**
      - **Plain radiographs of the spine** - limited role of diagnosis as misses up to 20% of vertebral metastasis
      - **Bone scan** - limited role of diagnosis as misses up to 20% of vertebral metastasis, and will miss paravertebral masses extending through neuroforamina
      - **MRI spine** - whole spine evaluated as 40% patients w/ metastatic cord compression are found to have asymptomatic epidermal metastasis elsewhere:
        - <u>Characteristic findings</u>:
          - Hypo-intensity on T1-weighted MRI - reduced signal relative to bone marrow signal signifies replacement by tumour
          - Gandalonium enhancement on T1 weighted MRI - may become isointense, i.e. deceptive normalisation of appearance by increasing intensity to relative to that of normal marrow

          
          ![](../img/epidural_spinal_cord_compression_of_breast_carcinoma.png)
        - <u>Role</u>:
          - Excellent anatomical resolution to distinguish similar causes such as osteomyelitis (usually crosses disc space and involves adjacent VBs)
          - Distinguish lesions from other masses (e.g. epidural abscess, tuberculoma, epidural haemorrhage)

2.  Spinal Epidural Abscess

    - **Definition** - abscess formation in the epidural space w/ the propensity of rapidly progressing into spinal cord compression which results in permanent sequelae
    - **Etiology of spinal epidural abscess:**
      - **Haematogenous spread from infections of other tissues:**
        - <u>Skin infection</u> - classically a/w furunculosis
        - <u>Soft tissue infection</u> - dental abscesses, pharyngeal abscesses, chronic sinusitis
        - <u>Others</u> - infective endocarditis, tuberculosis (Pott's disease)
      - **Direct extension of local infections:**
        - <u>Local predisposing conditions</u> - vertebral osteomyelitis, decubitus ulcers
        - <u>Predisposing procedures resulting in direct inoculation</u> - spinal surgery, lumbar puncture, epidural anaesthesia
    - **Risk factors of spinal epidural abscess:**
      - <u>Increased risk of transient bacteremia</u> - IVDU, active skin and soft tissue infection
      - <u>Increased risk of developing an infection</u> - i.e. immunocomprimised states:
        - HIV
        - Diabetes mellitus
        - CKD
        - Alcoholism
        - Malignancy
    - **Common microbial agents causing spinal epidural abscess:**
      - <u>GPC</u> - Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), Streptococcus
      - <u>GNB</u> - aerobes (e.g. enterobacter), anaerobes
      - <u>Others</u> - fungal or TB
    - **Clinical features of spinal epidural abscess:**
      - **Fever** - typical but not invariably present, but elevated WCC, ESR/CRP should raise suspicion
      - **Pain** - midline, aching back or neck pain is invariably present:
        - <u>Site</u> - back or neck +/- radicular pain (depending on site)
        - <u>Onset and progression</u> - usually acute or subacute (\< 2 weeks), but may occassionally be present for months or longer
        - <u>Provacating and relieving factors</u>:
          - Provoked by movement, coughing, or sneezing
          - Not relieved by rest (in fact characteristically awakens patients at night)
        - <u>Quality</u> - non-mechanical, aching quality
        - <u>Radiation</u> - midline and non-radiating, radiation occurs in concomitant radiculopathy
      - **Features of spinal cord compression** (myelopathy) - characteristically sensory Sx (e.g. parasthesia) before paralysis, and w/ early onset of 1) saddle parasthesia, 2) spastic paralysis, and 3) sphincteric disturbances:
        - Irreversible damage ensues once weakness and other myelopathic signs occur
        - Irreversible damage is partly due to abscess expansion but also venous congestion and thrombosis resulting in ischaemia
    - **Chronic sterile granulomatous abscess:**
      - Usually after treatment of acute epidural infection
      - Suspected w/ progression of compression despite initiation of Tx
    - **Ix:**
      - **Septic workup** - elevated WCC, ESR/CRP, and +ve blood culture in 50% cases (definitive microbiolgoical Dx through aspiration of abscess during surgery)
      - **MRI:**
        - <u>Role</u> - localisation of abscess and exclusion of other causes
        - <u>Characteristic findings</u>:
          - Diffuse gandalonium enhancement of epidural process in T1-weighted imaging
          - Hypointensity on T2-weighted imaging
      - **LP** - only indicated if suspected concomittent meningitis (\< 25%) or signs of encephalopathy:
        - <u>Risks</u> - iatrogenic meningitis (level of puncture planned to minimise passage of infected tissue into subdural space)
        - <u>Findings</u>:
          - WBC - neutrophylic pleocytosis
          - Protein - elevated
          - Glucose - reduced
          - Microbiology - cannot be cultured unless associated meningitis
    - **Mx:**
      - **Principles of Mx:**
        - <u>Spine intervetions</u> - decompressive laminectomy with aspiration for microbiological diagnosis and debridement
        - <u>Long-duration broad spectrum ABx</u> - empirical therapy requires coverage of GPC, GNB, and anaerobes
      - **Role of spine interventions** - main Tx of choice if not C/I unless abscess limited by spine or does not cause neurological impairment:
        - May prevent development of paralysis and improve or reverse paralysis in evolution
        - Unlikely to improve fixed deficits for more than several days
      - **Long duration broad spectrum ABx:**
        - <u>Duration</u> - up to 6-8 weeks
        - <u>Empirical ABx</u> - coverage of 1) GPC (Staphylococcus \[incl. MRSA\], streptococcus), 2) GNB, 3) anaerobes:
          - GPC coverage - vancomycin (15-20 mg/kg q12h)
          - GNR coverage - ceftriaxone (2g q12h)
          - Anaerobe coverage - metronidazole (30 mg/kg/d divided dose into q6h)
        - <u>Modification of ABx regimen</u> - guided by culture results (blood or surgical specimen)
    - **Prognosis** - significant recovery in two-thirds of patients w/ prompt Dx and Tx

3.  Spinal Epidural Hematoma

    - **Definition** - haemorrhage into the epidural (rarely subdural) spaces resulting in acute focal or radicular pain followed by variable evidence of spinal cord or conus medullaris disorders
    - **Risk factors of spinal epidural hematoma:**
      - <u>Traumatic</u> - trauma to the spine
      - <u>Bleeding tendancies</u> - therapeutic anticoagulation, tumors, haematological malignancies
      - <u>Iatrogenic</u> (exceedingly rare, negligible) - LP, epidural anaesthesia
    - **Clinical features of spinal epidural hematoma** - acute serial evolution of Sx w/ known risk factors:
      - <u>Pain</u> - acute focal or radicular pain initially
      - <u>Features of cord compression</u>:
        - Evidence of extradural spinal cord compression
        - Occasionally presents w/ Conus medullaris syndrome w/ relatively spared LL motor functions (absence of hemiplegia)
    - **Ix** - MRI or CT to delineate extent of bleeding (LP avoided)
    - **Mx:**
      - Reversal of underlying clotting disorder
      - Surgical decompression

4.  Hematomyelia

    - **Definition** - rare condition characterised by intramedullary haemorrhage, i.e. bleeding into the substance of the spinal cord
    - **Etiology of hematomyelia:**
      - Trauma
      - Intraparenchymal vascular malformation
      - Vasculitis (e.g. PAN, SLE)
      - Bleeding disorders
      - Spinal cord neoplasm
    - **Clinical features of hematomyelia:**
      - Painful transverse myelitis
      - Subarachnoid haemorrhage w/ large lesions extending into subarachnoid space
    - **Ix and Dx** - MRI or CT
    - **Mx:**
      - Supportive Mx
      - Surgery if caused by vascular malformation demonstrated by spinal angiography

5.  Acute Spondylytic Myelopathy

    - **Definition** - hypertensive injuries complicating subclinical degenerative C-spine diseases before it complicates w/ the syndrome of chronic spondylytic myelopathy
    - **Provoking stimulus of acute spondylytic myelopathy** - can be apparent or innoculous hyperextension injury:
      - Forward fall
      - Trauma associated w/ hyper-extension
      - Low-impact movement of the neck
    - **Pathophysiology of acute spondylytic myelopathy:**
      - <u>Pre-existing degenerative cervical spine disease</u> - asymptomatic, although patho-anatomically and radiologically already **stenotic spinal canal**
      - <u>Buckling of posterior ligamental flavum</u> - caused by hyper-extension injury, other rarer causes include acute disc herniation or sublaxation
    - **Clinical manifestations:**
      - Transient cord compression ("concussion" of the cord) w/ paraplegia, ocassionally in a central cord syndrome pattern
      - Deficits may occasionally be permanent

### Non-compressive myelopathies

- **DDx of non-compressive myelopathies:**
  - <u>Vascular</u> - acute spinal cord ischaemia
  - <u>Inflammatory myelopathies</u> - MS, NMOSD, CTDs, Bechet's disease, other vasculitides etc.
  - <u>Infectious causes</u>:
    - Infectious myelitis
    - Postinfectious transverse meylitis (likely immune-mediated like ADEM)
- **Differentiation between vascular/ischaemic from inflammatory/infectious causes** - clinical impression important as MRI and LP findings show significant overlap:
  - **Onset:**
    - <u>Vascular/ischaemic</u> - hyperacute presentation w/ back or neck pain and neurological findings
    - <u>Inflammatory/ infectious</u> - subacute presentation often a/w systemic Sx (e.g. fever)
  - **Examination findings:**
    - <u>Vascular/ ischaemic</u> - anterior pattern of SCI (striking retention of proprioceptive senses \[dissociative sensory loss\] due to sparing of dorsal columns)
    - <u>Inflammatory/ infectious</u> - systemic findings, neurology reflects complete transection
  - **Ix findings** - pleocytosis in CSF and focal enhancement on MRI are non-specific (oligoclonal bands and extensive longitudinal lesions are suggestive of inflammatory causes)
- **Myelopathy workup** - MRI, LP, and search for underlying systemic disease: 
![](../img/evaluation_of_myelopathy_harrisons.png)

1.  Spinal Cord Infarction

    - **Definition** - disorder caused by ischaemia and infarction of spinal cord tissue due to impaired perfusion in \>= 1 spinal arteries
    - **Vascular anatomy of the spinal cord** - supplied by single anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal artery coursing longituidinally over its surface:
      - <u>Anterior spinal artery</u> - singleton; courses vertically on the anterior surface of the cord:
        - Originates from paired branches of the **vertebral artery** at the craniocervical junction
        - Variably fed by **additional radicular vessels** at 1) C6, 2) upper thoracic level, and most consistently at 3) T11-L2 (**artery of Adamkiewicz**)
        - Paired penetrating vessels at each spinal cord segment to supply anterior two-thirds of the cord (not responsible for perfusion of dorsal columns)
      - <u>Posterior spinal artery</u> - paired; courses vertically on the posterior surface of the cord:
        - Branch of the **posterior inferior cerebellar artery** (PICA)
        - Less distinct below mid-thoracic level
        - Supplies posterior one-third of the cord (main supply of the posterior columns)
    - **Watershed area at level of T3-T4 and junction between ASA and PSA territories:**
      - Region of marginal blood flow to the upper thoracic segments with presence of ASA above and artery of Adamkiewicz below
      - Marginal blood flow in the junction between the two junctions of the two vascular territories
      - Infarction caused by hypoperfusion or cross-clamping of aorta results in myelopathy at T3-T4 segments or isolated infarction between the two vascular territories (profound spastic paralysis w/ minimal sensory loss)
    - **Causes of spinal cord syndrome** - no causes identifiable in most cases:
      - <u>Aortic pathology</u> - aortic atherosclerosis (thromboembolism), dissecting aortic aneurysm, aortic dissection w/ dissecting vertebral arteries, aortic surgery
      - <u>Cardioembolic occlusion</u> - e.g. AF, infective endocarditis
      - <u>Hyper-coagulable states</u>:
        - Anti-phospholipid syndrome
        - Connective tissue diseases (particularly SLE and Sjogren's syndrome)
      - <u>Others</u>:
        - Embolism of nucleus propolsus material into spinal vessel associated w/ local spine trauma
        - Surfer's myelopathy (prolonged extension and compression of ASA)
        - Drug induced (cocaine, heroin)
    - **Clinical manifestation of spinal cord infarction** - dependent on the teritory of ischaemia:
      - Anterior cord syndrome (ASA territory)
      - Posterior cord infarction (PSA territory)
      - Watershed zone infarction between ASA and PSA territory associated w/ generalised hypotension or cross-clamping of aorta)
    - **MRI spine** - may fail to demonstrate infarction in first days but imaging subsequently becomes abnormal:
      - <u>Diffusion-weighted imaging</u> - diffusion-weight restriction on DWI (decreased maximal signal reduction) over affected area
      - <u>T2 findings</u>:
        - Sagittal view - pencil like sign, i.e. longitudinally extensive anterior T2 hyperintesity
        - Axial view - owl's eyes, paired focal T2 hyperintensities in anterio-medial cord
    - **Mx:**
      - **Principles of Mx:**
        - <u>Anticoagulation</u> - reserved for APLS associated cord infarction (not used if presumed thromboembolism)
        - <u>BP elevation</u> - maintain MAP \> 90 mmHg +/- lumbar drainage of CSF reported to be helpful but not studied systematically

2.  Inflammatory and Immune Myelopathies (Myelitis)

    - **Definition and terminology:**
      - <u>Inflammatory myelopathy</u> - broad group of neuroinflammatory or autoimmune diseases resulting in spinal cord injury, including neuroinflammatory diseases, systemic inflammatory disorders, post-infectious myelitis, and acute infectious myelitis
      - <u>Transverse myelitis</u> - a clinical presentation reflecting a pattern of extensive spinal cord injury due to inflammation, clinically manifests as bilateral sensory Sx, unilateral or bilateral weakness, and bladder and/or bowel disturbances
    - **Epidemiology:**
      - Up to 25% cases remain idiopathic
      - The most common cause of inflammatory myelitis is multiple sclerosis
    - **DDx of inflammatory myelopathies:**
      - <u>Neuroinflammatory disorders</u> - multiple sclerosis, neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder,
      - <u>Systemic inflammatory disease</u>:
        - Sarcoidosis
        - Autoimmune diseases - systemic lupus erythematosus, Sjogren's syndrome, Anti-phospholipid syndrome, mixed connective tissue disease
        - Vasculitis - Polyarteritis nodosa, pANCA-associated vasculitis, primary CNS vasculitis, Behcet's disease
        - Chronic lymphocytic inflammation with potine perivascular enhancement responsive to steroids (CLIPPERS)
        - Erdheim-Chester disease
      - <u>Postinfectious myelitis</u> - CMV, mycoplasma, other viral exanthems
      - <u>Acute infectious myelitis</u> - viral most commonly described including HSV-1, HSV-2, EBV, CMV (+/- rabies)
    - **Multiple sclerosis** (MS-associated myelitis):
      - <u>Epidemiology</u>:
        - Most common cause of acute myelitis in the developed world (68% patients presenting w/ partial myelitis will have a definitive Dx of MS after mean FU of 4 years)
        - Acute myelitis (or opticospinal MS) is a ommon presentation of MS in individuals of Asian or African Ancestry
        - Rare in whites but may be associated w/ transverse myelitis
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u>:
        - Usually a partial myelitis (partial cord syndrome)
        - Transverse myelitis (see definition above) is rare
      - <u>Risk factors for conversion to MS in patient presenting w/ partial myelitis</u>:
        - Compatible demographic (\< 40y)
        - Inflammatory CSF with oligoclonal bands
        - \> 3 periventricular lesions on MRI brain
      - <u>Characteristic MRI findings</u>:
        - Mild swelling of the cord
        - Diffuse or multifocal "shoddy" areas of abnormal signals on T2-weighted sequences
        - Contrast enhancement of lesions suggests disruption of BBB in many cases
      - <u>Mx</u>:
        - Pulse steroids (IV methylprednisolone 500 mg qd for 3d followed by high dose oral prednisolone 1mg/kg/d for several weeks w/ gradual taper)
        - Plasmapharesis if refractory to steroids
    - **Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder** (NMOSD-associated myelitis):
      - <u>Clinical associations</u> - SLE and other autoimmune phenomenon
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u>:
        - Severe myeloopathy (often transverse myelitis) often associated w/ bilateral optic neuritis preceding or following myelitis by weeks or months
        - Recurrent myelitis w/o optic nerve or other involvement is possible
      - <u>Characteristic MRI findings</u> - extensive longitudinal lesions spanning \>=3 vertebral segments (see notes on NMOSD)
      - <u>Other Ix</u>:
        - Serology - anti-AQP-4 (90%), anti-MOG
      - <u>Mx</u> - glucocorticoids (+/- plasmapharesis) for induction; biologics for maintenance
    - **Sarcoidosis** (Sarcoid myelopathy or neurosarcoidosis):
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - absence of systmeic manifestation of sarcoid in up to 50% cases:
        - Rarely a transverse myelitis
        - Sensory involvement often predominates (dorsal surface involvement)
      - <u>Characteristic MRI findings</u>:
        - Edematous swelling of the spinal cord
        - Ocassionally longitudinal extensive involvement mimicking NMO
        - Subpial gandolinium enhancement of active lesions on dorsal surfaces (trident sign on axial view)
      - <u>Characteristic CSF findings</u>:
        - Mild lymphocytic pleocytosis
        - Elevated protein
        - Hypoglycorrhachia (not always present but useful for distinguishing neurosarcoid from other causes)
        - Oligoclonal bands (not always prseent)
        - CSF ACEi (low Sn and Sp)
      - <u>Other Ix</u> - diagnostic evaluation of sarcoid if suspicious
      - <u>Mx</u> - high-dose steroid + steroid sparing agent +/- biologics

3.  High-Voltage Electrical Injury

